window.EXAM={"id": "exam-1", "name": "Practice Exam 1", "stimuli": {"S1": {"title": "Questions 1–3 refer to the following information.", "html": "<p>A student's laptop is running slowly while the student edits a video for a class project. The student opens Task Manager and records the readings below.</p>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Reading</th></tr>\n<tr><td>CPU</td><td>97% (sustained for the last 15 minutes)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory (RAM)</td><td>58% of 16 GB in use</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Storage (C:)</td><td>212 GB free of 512 GB</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wi-Fi</td><td>Connected, 4 of 4 bars</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temperature warnings</td><td>None</td></tr></table>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Top processes</th><th>Status</th><th>CPU</th></tr>\n<tr><td>ClipStudio Export</td><td>Not responding</td><td>81%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Web browser (14 tabs)</td><td>Running</td><td>9%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Music player</td><td>Running</td><td>3%</td></tr></table>"}, "S2": {"title": "Questions 14–16 refer to the following information.", "html": "<p>The receptionist's desktop at Maple Street Dental cannot open any websites. Every other computer in the office works normally and has an address in the 192.168.10.0/24 network. A technician records the following.</p>\n<pre>C:\\&gt; ipconfig\nEthernet adapter Ethernet:\n   Autoconfiguration IPv4 Address . . : 169.254.37.112\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.0.0\n   Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . :\n\nC:\\&gt; ping 8.8.8.8\nPinging 8.8.8.8 with 32 bytes of data:\nPING: transmit failed. General failure.\nPING: transmit failed. General failure.\nPING: transmit failed. General failure.\nPING: transmit failed. General failure.\n    Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 0, Lost = 4 (100% loss)</pre>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Office router indicator</th><th>Status</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Power</td><td>Steady green</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Internet</td><td>Steady green</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LAN port 3 (receptionist desktop)</td><td>Flashing green</td></tr></table>"}, "S3": {"title": "Questions 17–18 refer to the following information.", "html": "<p>The owner of a two-person photography business works from a 1,400-square-foot apartment. The network has two laptops, one desktop, a network-attached storage (NAS) device, a printer, and a smart TV. The owner's budget for new equipment is $300. The owner asked an AI tool for a network design and received the following suggestion.</p>\n<div class=\"ai\"><b>AI-generated suggestion</b><ol>\n<li>Install an equipment rack with two 48-port managed switches and a dedicated hardware firewall appliance (estimated cost: $2,400).</li>\n<li>Connect the NAS to the wireless network with a dynamic (DHCP) address so that it can be moved between rooms.</li>\n<li>Use one wireless router with WPA3 encryption, placed in a central location.</li></ol></div>\n<p>The owner plans to use the NAS as a media and file server that every device on the network can reach.</p>"}, "S4": {"title": "Questions 30–32 refer to the following information.", "html": "<p>A technician at Westbrook Public Library is dividing the network 192.168.40.0/24 into subnets. The plan is shown below.</p>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Hosts needed</th><th>Proposed subnet</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Staff workstations</td><td>50</td><td>192.168.40.0/26</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Public computers</td><td>25</td><td>192.168.40.64/27</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Catalog kiosks and printers</td><td>10</td><td>192.168.40.96/28</td></tr></table>"}, "S5": {"title": "Questions 33–35 refer to the following information.", "html": "<p>Northgate Clinic's internal firewall applies the following access control list (ACL) to traffic entering the server segment (10.20.5.0/24). The staff segment is 10.20.2.0/24, and the IT administrator segment is 10.20.1.0/24.</p>\n<pre>Rule  Action  Direction  Protocol  Port  Source\n101   DENY    inbound    TCP       ALL   10.20.0.0/16\n102   ALLOW   inbound    TCP       443   10.20.2.0/24\n103   ALLOW   inbound    TCP       22    10.20.1.0/24\n104   ALLOW   inbound    TCP       23    10.20.1.0/24\n105   DENY    inbound    TCP       ALL   ALL</pre>\n<p>After the ACL is applied, staff report that they can no longer open the patient-records web application (HTTPS) on server 10.20.5.10, and IT administrators report that their SSH sessions to the server fail.</p>"}, "S6": {"title": "Questions 46–47 refer to the following information.", "html": "<p>A student at a high school whose default gateway is 10.1.0.1 reports that an online learning platform is slow. The student runs the following command.</p>\n<pre>C:\\&gt; tracert learn.example.org\nTracing route to learn.example.org [198.51.100.24]\nover a maximum of 30 hops:\n  1     1 ms     1 ms     1 ms  10.1.0.1\n  2     8 ms     7 ms     9 ms  203.0.113.1\n  3    11 ms    10 ms    12 ms  203.0.113.45\n  4   212 ms   198 ms   225 ms  192.0.2.17\n  5   215 ms   209 ms   220 ms  192.0.2.90\n  6   218 ms   221 ms   214 ms  198.51.100.24\nTrace complete.</pre>"}, "S7": {"title": "Questions 48–50 refer to the following information.", "html": "<p>Lakeside Credit Union is open 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. The staff segment is 10.30.4.0/24, and the member-records server is 10.30.0.12. The following entries come from the intrusion detection system (IDS) and switch logs for one Saturday. The address 203.0.113.66 appears on a threat-intelligence list of known malicious addresses.</p>\n<pre>Time      Source          Destination      Event\n02:14:07  10.30.4.77      10.30.0.0/24     Port scan detected (TCP 1-1024)\n02:16:41  10.30.4.77      10.30.0.12       SSH login failed (user: admin) x48\n02:19:03  10.30.4.77      10.30.0.12       SSH login succeeded (user: backup)\n02:22:15  10.30.0.12      203.0.113.66     Outbound transfer 2.3 GB (TCP 443)\n09:05:30  switch-2        Gi0/3            Input errors and packet drops increasing</pre>"}}, "questions": [{"id": 1, "lo": "1.1.B", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "Based on the readings, which of the following is the most likely root cause of the slow performance?", "opts": ["Insufficient available storage", "A weak wireless signal", "Insufficient memory", "An overloaded processor"], "ans": "D", "expl": "CPU use above 80–90% for a sustained period means the processor is overloaded, and one process is using 81% while not responding. Storage is 41% free (well above 10%), RAM is 58%, and the signal is 4 of 4 bars.", "set": "S1"}, {"id": 2, "lo": "1.1.C", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "Which of the following is the most appropriate first solution for the student to implement?", "opts": ["Install additional RAM in the laptop", "End the ClipStudio Export process that is not responding", "Move the laptop closer to the wireless access point", "Replace the storage drive with a larger drive"], "ans": "B", "expl": "The solution for high CPU use is to end the high-usage or unresponsive task. It is simple, free, and nondisruptive, so it is tried first. RAM and storage are not the problem, and the signal is already strong.", "set": "S1"}, {"id": 3, "lo": "1.1.A", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "After implementing the solution, which of the following should the student do next?", "opts": ["Replace the processor, because the CPU reading was above 90%", "Ask an AI tool whether the problem is fixed instead of testing the laptop", "Verify that applications respond normally and that the export can be completed", "Begin the troubleshooting process again by collecting user reports"], "ans": "C", "expl": "After a solution is implemented, it must be verified by confirming that the device functions normally. Hardware replacement is a last resort, AI output must be verified by hand, and starting over is only needed if verification fails.", "set": "S1"}, {"id": 4, "lo": "1.2.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A student selects the SSID of a password-protected wireless network and types the password, but the device reports that the password is incorrect. The student is certain that every character is correct. Which of the following is the most likely reason the connection failed?", "opts": ["One or more letters have different capitalization, because wireless passwords are case-sensitive.", "The network uses WPA3 encryption, which does not allow devices to join with a password.", "The device's wireless adapter is turned off, so the password is never sent to the network.", "The SSID is hidden, so the network rejects every password, even one that is correct."], "ans": "A", "expl": "Wireless network passwords are case-sensitive, so the same letters with different capitalization are a different password. WPA3 networks accept passwords, an adapter that is off would not show the SSID list, and a hidden SSID does not reject a correct password.", "set": null}, {"id": 5, "lo": "1.2.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A student plays an online game on a desktop computer in a basement room. The wireless router is two floors above, the computer shows 1 of 4 bars of signal, and the game lags. The room has an Ethernet wall port connected to the router. Which of the following actions would most improve the reliability of the connection?", "opts": ["Closing unused browser tabs on the computer", "Connecting the computer to the Ethernet wall port", "Deleting temporary files from the computer", "Disconnecting a smart speaker from the network"], "ans": "B", "expl": "A weak signal causes lag, and switching to a wired Ethernet connection when one is available removes the distance and wall interference problem. Closing tabs and deleting files help device performance, not signal strength; disconnecting one smart speaker frees very little bandwidth.", "set": null}, {"id": 6, "lo": "1.3.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "An employee at a tutoring center opens an email attachment, and the front-desk computer becomes infected with malware. Which of the following is a likely impact of the infection?", "opts": ["The wireless network's encryption could be changed from WPA3 to WEP without any warning.", "The computer's hardcoded MAC address could be permanently replaced with a different one.", "The internet service provider could be forced to assign the center a new public IP address.", "An adversary could monitor activity on the computer and use it to spread malware."], "ans": "D", "expl": "Malware can let attackers access files, monitor activity, take remote control of a device, and use it to spread to other systems. The other options describe events that malware on one computer does not cause.", "set": null}, {"id": 7, "lo": "1.3.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "Employees at a travel agency receive urgent emails that appear to come from the agency's bank. The emails link to a fake sign-in page. Which of the following security controls would best limit the impact of these messages?", "opts": ["Installing privacy screens on all employee monitors at the agency", "Requiring employees to restart their computers at the end of each day", "Positioning employee screens away from high-traffic areas of the office", "Enabling email filtering and training employees to report phishing"], "ans": "D", "expl": "The emails are phishing. The matching controls are email filtering to block malicious messages and training users to recognize and report them. Privacy screens and screen positioning address shoulder surfing.", "set": null}, {"id": 8, "lo": "1.4.A", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A student is creating a password for a school account. Which of the following passwords best follows strong password guidance?", "opts": ["sunshine2009", "Rover1985!", "T7#qmV!r2pLx", "PASSWORD!!!!"], "ans": "C", "expl": "T7#qmV!r2pLx is 12 characters long, uses all four character sets, and contains no words or personal information. Rover1985! uses a pet name and a birth year, sunshine2009 is a dictionary word with only two sets, and PASSWORD!!!! is a common word.", "set": null}, {"id": 9, "lo": "1.4.B", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A student wants to check grades on the school portal while using a coffee shop's open wireless network. Which of the following practices best reduces the risk of credential exposure?", "opts": ["Turning on automatic connection to open networks so the device reconnects quickly", "Using the same password as other accounts so that it is easier to remember", "Turning off automatic updates to save bandwidth on the public network", "Connecting through a virtual private network (VPN) and confirming that the site uses HTTPS"], "ans": "D", "expl": "On untrusted networks, users should connect through a secure network such as a VPN and make sure websites use HTTPS, which encrypts traffic. Automatic connection to open networks, password reuse, and disabled updates all increase risk.", "set": null}, {"id": 10, "lo": "1.4.C", "skill": "2.D", "stem": "A family is configuring a new wireless router. Which of the following configurations provides the strongest wireless security?", "opts": ["WPA3 encryption with WPS disabled", "WPA2 encryption with Wi-Fi Protected Setup (WPS) enabled", "WEP encryption with a 26-character key", "WPA encryption with the SSID hidden"], "ans": "A", "expl": "WPA3 is the recommended encryption and WPS should be disabled. WPA2 is acceptable only as a minimum, and leaving WPS on weakens it. WEP and WPA are insecure regardless of key length or a hidden SSID.", "set": null}, {"id": 11, "lo": "2.1.A", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "The light for port 2 on a small office switch is unlit, while the lights for the other connected ports are steady green. The computer connected to port 2 has no network connectivity. Which of the following is the most likely cause?", "opts": ["The DHCP server has run out of addresses to assign.", "The office's internet service provider is experiencing an outage.", "The computer's wireless signal is too weak to connect.", "The cable or port for that computer is faulty or unplugged."], "ans": "D", "expl": "An unlit indicator light usually means a faulty port or cable or an unplugged cable. The other ports are working, which rules out an ISP outage; DHCP problems do not turn off a link light; and the computer is wired, not wireless.", "set": null}, {"id": 12, "lo": "2.2.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "The MAC address of a network printer is 3C:52:A1:0F:7E:19. Which of the following correctly describes this address?", "opts": ["The first half, 3C:52:A1, identifies the manufacturer, and the second half identifies the specific device.", "The address changes each time the printer is moved to a different network.", "The address is a 32-bit logical address assigned to the printer by a DHCP server.", "The first half identifies the network, and the second half identifies the host on that network."], "ans": "A", "expl": "A MAC address is a 48-bit identifier hardcoded into the NIC. The first half is the Organizationally Unique Identifier (OUI) of the manufacturer, and the second half identifies the device. It stays the same on every network. A 32-bit logical address and a network/host split describe IPv4 addresses, not MAC addresses.", "set": null}, {"id": 13, "lo": "2.2.B", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A device is configured with the IP address 172.20.14.9 and the subnet mask 255.255.0.0. Which of the following is the network ID portion of the address?", "opts": ["172", "172.20", "172.20.14", "172.20.14.9"], "ans": "B", "expl": "The mask 255.255.0.0 marks the first two octets as network bits, so the network ID is 172.20 and the last two octets (14.9) are host bits.", "set": null}, {"id": 14, "lo": "2.2.C", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "Which of the following best describes the address 169.254.37.112 shown in the ipconfig output?", "opts": ["It is a public address that the office's internet service provider assigned to the desktop.", "It is a loopback address that the desktop uses to send test data only to itself.", "It is a static private address that was manually configured for the office network.", "It is an APIPA address the desktop assigned itself because no DHCP server responded."], "ans": "D", "expl": "Addresses from 169.254.0.0 to 169.254.255.255 are Automatic Private IP Addressing (APIPA) addresses, which a host uses when the DHCP server is unavailable or misconfigured. Loopback is 127.0.0.1, and the office's private network is 192.168.10.0/24.", "set": "S2"}, {"id": 15, "lo": "2.1.A", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "Based on all of the evidence, which of the following is the most likely root cause of the problem?", "opts": ["The office's internet connection is down.", "The Ethernet cable to the desktop is damaged.", "The desktop did not receive an IP configuration from the DHCP server.", "The public server at 8.8.8.8 is not responding to requests."], "ans": "C", "expl": "The APIPA address and the empty default gateway show the desktop never received its IP configuration. The Internet light is green and other computers work (so the connection is up), the port light is flashing green (so the cable link is up), and the ping never left the desktop.", "set": "S2"}, {"id": 16, "lo": "2.1.B", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "Which of the following should the technician try first to restore the desktop's connectivity?", "opts": ["Run ipconfig /release and then ipconfig /renew on the desktop", "Replace the office router with a new wireless gateway", "Enable the desktop's Wi-Fi adapter and restart the desktop", "Plug the desktop's cable into a different port on the router"], "ans": "A", "expl": "For a device with an APIPA address, the matching solutions are disconnecting and reconnecting or releasing and renewing the IP address. Enabling the adapter and restarting is the fix for a loopback-only address; changing ports is for unlit or red lights; replacing the router is costly and unnecessary.", "set": "S2"}, {"id": 17, "lo": "2.4.A", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "Which of the following best evaluates item 1 of the AI-generated suggestion?", "opts": ["It is technically workable but overcomplicated and far over budget for a network of six devices.", "It is appropriate, because enterprise equipment is always more reliable than consumer equipment.", "It is incorrect, because managed switches cannot connect to a NAS device.", "It is appropriate, because extra switch ports allow the business to grow in the future."], "ans": "A", "expl": "AI suggestions can be technically accurate yet unrealistic or overcomplicated for a SOHO network. Two 48-port switches and a rack for six devices cost eight times the budget and add management work a two-person business does not need.", "set": "S3"}, {"id": 18, "lo": "2.4.B", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "Which of the following changes to item 2 is most appropriate for the way the owner plans to use the NAS?", "opts": ["Connect the NAS with a wired Ethernet cable and assign it a static IP address.", "Keep the NAS on wireless but shorten its DHCP lease so it renews more often.", "Move the NAS to a guest network so that visitors can also reach it.", "Turn off the file-sharing software on the NAS to improve its security."], "ans": "A", "expl": "A media server should have a wired connection for speed and reliability and a static IP address or hostname so other devices can always find it. A changing address breaks that, a guest network exposes it, and file-sharing software is required for it to serve files.", "set": "S3"}, {"id": 19, "lo": "2.3.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A print shop is adding wired workstations next to large HVAC units and banks of fluorescent lights. Each cable run is less than 40 meters, and the budget is limited. Which of the following transmission media is most appropriate?", "opts": ["Coaxial cable", "Fiber optic cable", "Shielded twisted pair (STP)", "Unshielded twisted pair (UTP)"], "ans": "C", "expl": "STP is affordable, easy to install, supports up to 10 Gbps over 100 meters, and is the choice for environments with high EMI such as HVAC equipment and fluorescent lighting. UTP is fine for standard installations, fiber is immune to EMI but expensive and not common inside SOHO LANs, and coaxial is used mainly where it already exists.", "set": null}, {"id": 20, "lo": "2.3.C", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A home office network has the devices listed below. Which of the following devices is most appropriately configured with a static IP address?", "opts": ["A visitor's smartphone that connects once a month", "A laptop that a family member takes to school each day", "A tablet that is used in different rooms of the house", "A security camera recorder that the owner views remotely"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Static addressing fits devices that need a consistent address, such as servers, printers, remote services, and security systems. Mobile and temporary devices such as phones, laptops, tablets, and guest devices are better served by dynamic addressing.", "set": null}, {"id": 21, "lo": "2.5.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "A café owner installs a new wireless router and leaves the administrator username and password set to the factory defaults. Which of the following is the most likely impact?", "opts": ["An adversary could sign in to the router and change settings or disable security controls.", "The router could stop installing firmware updates, because default settings block updates.", "Customers' devices could receive APIPA addresses instead of addresses from the private range.", "The router could be limited to WEP encryption until the default credentials are changed."], "ans": "A", "expl": "Default usernames and passwords are widely known, so anyone who reaches the router's login page could take control of it, change configurations, or disable security controls. The other choices are not effects of default credentials.", "set": null}, {"id": 22, "lo": "2.5.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "A family adds smart plugs, a video doorbell, and a smart thermostat to its home network. The family's laptops store tax records. Which of the following controls best limits the risk that a compromised smart device could be used to reach the laptops?", "opts": ["Assigning each smart device a static IP address from the router's range", "Moving the wireless router closer to the smart devices in the home", "Placing the smart devices on a separate network segment from the laptops", "Enabling WPS so that the smart devices can join the network more easily"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Isolating IoT devices on a separate segment from critical devices and data limits lateral movement. Static addresses and router placement do not restrict access, and WPS should be disabled because it weakens wireless security.", "set": null}, {"id": 23, "lo": "2.6.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "Which of the following best explains how a firewall placed between a guest wireless segment and an internal office segment improves security?", "opts": ["It applies rules on IP addresses, ports, and protocols that keep guest traffic out of internal systems.", "It encrypts all traffic on the guest network so that guests cannot read one another's data.", "It assigns every guest device a public IP address so that each guest device can be tracked.", "It increases the bandwidth available to internal devices by slowing down guest devices."], "ans": "A", "expl": "Firewalls limit the traffic allowed into or out of a network by evaluating IP addresses, ports, and protocols. Between zones, that keeps guests from reaching internal systems and stops malware or adversaries from moving laterally. Encryption is a wireless setting, not a firewall function.", "set": null}, {"id": 24, "lo": "2.6.B", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A home network will be divided into three segments based on risk level: a high-security segment, an IoT segment, and a guest wireless segment. Which of the following devices should be placed in the IoT segment?", "opts": ["A home office computer that stores client invoices", "A visitor's smartphone", "A smart thermostat", "A NAS that stores family photo backups"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Grouping by risk places often-vulnerable IoT devices such as thermostats, speakers, and smart TVs together. The office computer and NAS belong in the high-security segment, and the visitor's phone belongs on the guest wireless segment.", "set": null}, {"id": 25, "lo": "2.6.B", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A family wants online tournament play on a gaming console and 4K streaming from a media server to receive priority over other household traffic. Which of the following describes the basis for grouping these two devices together?", "opts": ["Grouping by risk level", "Grouping by type of user", "Grouping by performance needs", "Grouping by device manufacturer"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Devices can be grouped by performance needs to prioritize high-bandwidth or low-latency traffic, such as gaming consoles and media servers used for streaming. Risk-level and user-type grouping serve security and access control goals instead.", "set": null}, {"id": 26, "lo": "3.1.A", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "An esports club is choosing devices for players in a tournament that requires high frame rates and low input latency. Which of the following devices is most appropriate for the players?", "opts": ["Basic laptops", "Gaming desktops", "Smartphones", "Tablets"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Gaming consoles, desktops, or high-performance laptops are needed where high frame rates and low input latency are required. Tablets and smartphones suit mobile access to forms and dashboards, and basic laptops suit browser-based multitasking.", "set": null}, {"id": 27, "lo": "3.1.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A conference hall expects 140 attendees to connect smartphones in one open room. The event planner assumes that each wireless access point (WAP) can support 40 mobile devices. What is the minimum number of WAPs needed?", "opts": ["3", "4", "5", "7"], "ans": "B", "expl": "140 ÷ 40 = 3.5, and a partial WAP is not possible, so the hall needs 4 WAPs. Three WAPs would support only 120 devices.", "set": null}, {"id": 28, "lo": "3.1.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "An event network needs 52 wired devices. Each available switch has 24 ports, and one port on each switch is reserved as an uplink to the router. What is the minimum number of switches needed?", "opts": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Each switch has 24 − 1 = 23 ports for devices. Two switches give 46 ports, which is not enough; three switches give 69 ports, which covers 52 devices.", "set": null}, {"id": 29, "lo": "3.2.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A company's research lab requires the strongest possible isolation of its testing network from the rest of the company's network, and the budget allows the purchase of additional hardware. Which of the following segmentation methods is most appropriate?", "opts": ["Creating a separate SSID on the existing wireless access point", "Assigning the lab's ports to a new VLAN on the existing switch", "Creating a new subnet on the existing switch and router", "Building a physically separate network with its own switch and router"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Physical segmentation uses separate switches, routers, or cabling and should be used when maximum isolation is needed. It costs more, but the budget allows it. VLANs, SSIDs, and subnets are logical separations on shared hardware.", "set": null}, {"id": 30, "lo": "3.4.C", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "Which of the following is the subnet mask for the public computers subnet?", "opts": ["255.255.255.192", "255.255.255.224", "255.255.255.240", "255.255.255.248"], "ans": "B", "expl": "/27 means 27 network bits: 24 ones in the first three octets and 3 more in the fourth (11100000 = 224), so the mask is 255.255.255.224. /26 is .192, /28 is .240, and /29 is .248.", "set": "S4"}, {"id": 31, "lo": "3.4.C", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "The library plans to grow the staff segment to 70 hosts. What is the smallest subnet size that can accommodate the staff segment?", "opts": ["/24", "/25", "/26", "/27"], "ans": "B", "expl": "70 hosts plus the network and broadcast addresses need 72 addresses. The next power of 2 is 128 (7 host bits), which is a /25 with 126 usable hosts. A /26 has only 62 usable hosts.", "set": "S4"}, {"id": 32, "lo": "3.3.B", "skill": "1.D", "stem": "A public computer at 192.168.40.90/27 sends a print job to a kiosk printer at 192.168.40.101/28. Which of the following best describes how the computer sends the data?", "opts": ["It sends the data directly to the printer's MAC address, because both addresses begin with 192.168.40.", "It broadcasts the data to every subnet, because the masks of the two devices differ.", "It cannot send the data, because private addresses cannot be routed between subnets.", "It sends the data to its default gateway, because the printer is on a different subnet."], "ans": "D", "expl": "With a /27 mask, the computer's network is 192.168.40.64–95, and .101 falls outside it (the printer's /28 block is .96–.111). Because the network bits differ, the computer sends the data to its default gateway, which routes it to the other subnet.", "set": "S4"}, {"id": 33, "lo": "3.5.C", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "Which of the following best explains why staff can no longer reach the patient-records application?", "opts": ["Rule 102 uses the wrong port, because HTTPS uses port 80.", "Rule 105 overrides every earlier rule, because the last rule in an ACL has the highest priority.", "Rule 102 is ignored, because firewall rules cannot filter traffic by port number.", "Rule 101 matches staff traffic first, because 10.20.2.0/24 falls within 10.20.0.0/16."], "ans": "D", "expl": "ACLs are checked from top to bottom, and the first matching rule is executed. Every staff and admin address is inside 10.20.0.0/16, so rule 101 denies the traffic before rules 102 and 103 are reached. HTTPS uses port 443, and the last rule applies only to traffic no earlier rule matched.", "set": "S5"}, {"id": 34, "lo": "3.6.C", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "Which of the following changes would restore staff HTTPS access and administrator SSH access while keeping deny-by-default protection?", "opts": ["Delete rule 105.", "Change the source in rule 102 to ALL.", "Add rule 106 to allow TCP port 443 from 10.20.2.0/24.", "Move rule 101 below rules 102, 103, and 104."], "ans": "D", "expl": "The fix for this kind of problem is to reorder the ACL so specific allow rules sit above broad deny rules. A new rule 106 would never be reached, deleting rule 105 removes the final deny, and allowing port 443 from ALL is broader than needed and still blocked by rule 101.", "set": "S5"}, {"id": 35, "lo": "3.5.C", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "Which rule should be removed because it permits a vulnerable, unencrypted protocol?", "opts": ["Rule 102", "Rule 103", "Rule 104", "Rule 105"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Rule 104 allows port 23, Telnet, which sends remote CLI sessions unencrypted. Administrators already have SSH on port 22 through rule 103, so Telnet should be closed.", "set": "S5"}, {"id": 36, "lo": "3.2.C", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A hospital's core network must keep communicating even if any single link fails, and the hospital has a dedicated network support team. Which of the following topologies is most appropriate?", "opts": ["Bus", "Mesh", "Ring", "Star"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Mesh provides multiple paths, so communication continues when one connection fails. It needs more cabling and management, which a dedicated support team can handle. A star's central switch is a single point of failure, and bus and ring are avoided for their limited fault tolerance.", "set": null}, {"id": 37, "lo": "3.2.D", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A school asked an AI tool for a segmented LAN plan for staff, students, and visiting guests. The tool suggested VLAN 10 for staff, VLAN 20 for students, and one SSID named School-WiFi for all wireless users. Which of the following is the most significant problem with the suggestion?", "opts": ["It uses VLAN numbers that are not multiples of 100.", "It places staff and students on different VLANs.", "It provides no separate segment for guest users.", "It uses VLANs instead of a mesh topology."], "ans": "C", "expl": "AI suggestions may leave out segments or meet only some requirements. Guests must be isolated from internal resources, but the plan has no guest segment, and a single SSID for everyone puts guests on the same wireless network as staff and students.", "set": null}, {"id": 38, "lo": "3.3.A", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "A technician is manually configuring a printer on the network 192.168.8.0/24. The router's address is 192.168.8.1. Which of the following configurations is valid for the printer?", "opts": ["IP 192.168.8.1, mask 255.255.255.0, gateway 192.168.8.1", "IP 192.168.9.50, mask 255.255.255.0, gateway 192.168.8.1", "IP 192.168.8.255, mask 255.255.255.0, gateway 192.168.8.1", "IP 192.168.8.50, mask 255.255.255.0, gateway 192.168.8.1"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Every IP address must be unique and within the network's range. .1 is already the router, .255 is the broadcast address, and 192.168.9.50 is on a different network than its gateway.", "set": null}, {"id": 39, "lo": "3.3.D", "skill": "1.D", "stem": "A technician enables a guest network on a wireless access point. Which of the following tests best verifies that the guest network is configured correctly?", "opts": ["Pinging the internal file server from a computer that is connected to the internal network", "Checking that the guest SSID appears in the list of available networks on a nearby laptop", "Connecting a device to the guest SSID and checking that it reaches the internet but not internal servers", "Confirming that the internal SSID is configured with WPA3 encryption and a strong password"], "ans": "C", "expl": "A correctly configured guest network gives internet access without access to internal resources, so the test must check both from a guest-connected device. The other choices check only that the network exists or test the internal network instead.", "set": null}, {"id": 40, "lo": "3.4.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "An unknown laptop is plugged into an unused wall jack in a conference room at an accounting firm and receives an address on the internal network. Which of the following is the most likely impact if this device is malicious?", "opts": ["It could force the switch to stop forwarding all traffic to every port.", "It could cause every device on the network to receive an APIPA address.", "It could intercept traffic, spread malware, or bypass internal security controls.", "It could change the wireless encryption on the access points to WEP."], "ans": "C", "expl": "Unknown or unverified devices allowed onto a network may intercept traffic, spread malware, or bypass internal security controls. Controls include MAC filtering, setting unused ports to down, and DHCP reservations.", "set": null}, {"id": 41, "lo": "3.5.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "A company needs a firewall for a high-risk zone that requires intrusion prevention and filtering based on the application in use. Which of the following firewalls is most appropriate?", "opts": ["Host-based firewall", "Next-generation firewall (NGFW)", "Stateful firewall", "Stateless firewall"], "ans": "B", "expl": "An NGFW adds intrusion prevention and application-based filtering and suits high-risk zones. A stateless firewall uses fixed rules, a stateful firewall tracks and logs connections but does not filter by application, and a host-based firewall protects a single device.", "set": null}, {"id": 42, "lo": "3.6.A", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "A technician is documenting a segmented LAN to support future troubleshooting. Which of the following should the documentation include?", "opts": ["A labeled diagram, consistent device names, the IP scheme with static devices marked, and each segment's firewall rules", "A list of device brands and purchase dates, leaving out IP addresses so the document never needs to be updated", "The wireless passwords for every SSID, printed on a copy of the network diagram posted in the public lobby", "A photo of the equipment rack in place of a diagram, because a photo shows the real physical connections"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Good documentation has a labeled diagram of wired and wireless connections, consistent device names and roles, the IP scheme with static devices noted, and segments with special firewall rules. It must be updated whenever something changes, and it should never expose passwords.", "set": null}, {"id": 43, "lo": "3.6.B", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "On Monday morning, 15 laptops in an office's new wing show addresses beginning with 169.254, while devices that connected earlier work normally. The DHCP pool for that segment is 192.168.20.100 to 192.168.20.149, and all 50 addresses are leased. Which of the following is the most likely root cause?", "opts": ["A firewall rule is blocking DNS traffic from the segment.", "The DHCP pool for the segment is exhausted.", "The wireless signal in the new wing is too weak.", "The default gateway on the router is configured incorrectly."], "ans": "B", "expl": "An exhausted DHCP pool causes new devices to be assigned APIPA addresses. All 50 addresses are leased, devices already connected work, and only the newly arriving laptops fail.", "set": null}, {"id": 44, "lo": "4.1.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "After a weekend, a hardware store finds that 300 product prices in its inventory database were changed. The database log shows that the changes were made from the account of an employee who was on vacation. Which part of the CIA triad was most clearly compromised?", "opts": ["Integrity, because records were altered without authorization", "Availability, because the database could not be reached over the weekend", "Confidentiality, because the employee's vacation schedule was exposed", "Integrity, because the database log was deleted to hide the changes"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Integrity means data are accurate and unaltered, and unauthorized data changes are evidence of an integrity breach. Nothing shows the database was down, the vacation schedule is not the exposed data, and the log was not deleted (it is the evidence).", "set": null}, {"id": 45, "lo": "4.1.B", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "Which of the following network outages would most likely have the greatest impact on a regional food distributor?", "opts": ["A 15-minute outage of the ordering system at 3:00 a.m. on a Sunday", "A 15-minute outage of the guest wireless network in the lobby", "A 15-minute outage of a test server that is used for training", "A 15-minute outage of the ordering system during peak weekday order processing"], "ans": "D", "expl": "The impact of a lost connection depends on timing and on the system affected. Outages during peak activity cause greater impacts, and the ordering system is essential to the business, while the guest Wi-Fi and training server are not.", "set": null}, {"id": 46, "lo": "4.4.C", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "Which of the following is the best interpretation of the tracert output?", "opts": ["The path has 6 hops, and the delay begins at the school's default gateway.", "The path has 6 hops, and the delay begins at hop 4, outside the school's network.", "The path has 5 hops, and the delay occurs only at the destination server.", "The path has 3 hops, and the delay begins at the ISP router at hop 2."], "ans": "B", "expl": "Each line is a hop, and the destination is reached at hop 6. Response times are low through hop 3 and jump to about 200 ms at hop 4 (192.0.2.17), staying high afterward, so the delay starts there, beyond the school's gateway and its ISP's first routers.", "set": "S6"}, {"id": 47, "lo": "4.4.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "Hop 1 is the school's default gateway. Which of the following best describes what the gateway does to the student's outgoing packets before forwarding them to hop 2?", "opts": ["It replaces the destination IP address with the student computer's MAC address.", "It replaces the private source IP address with the gateway's public IP address.", "It encrypts the packets with WPA3 so that they can travel across the internet.", "It assigns the packets a permanent source IP address from the 169.254.0.0 range."], "ans": "B", "expl": "Network address translation (NAT) lets devices with private addresses (10.1.x.x) communicate with external devices. The gateway replaces the source address with its single external address and reverses the translation for return traffic.", "set": "S6"}, {"id": 48, "lo": "4.5.C", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "Which of the following log entries is an indicator of a network performance or configuration issue rather than an indicator of a security threat?", "opts": ["The 02:14:07 port scan", "The 02:16:41 failed logins", "The 02:22:15 outbound transfer", "The 09:05:30 input errors and packet drops"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Frequent packet drops and errors suggest a technical problem, such as a failing cable or port, not necessarily an adversary. Unauthorized scans, repeated failed logins, and connections to known malicious addresses are threat indicators.", "set": "S7"}, {"id": 49, "lo": "4.1.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "The 02:22:15 entry most directly provides evidence that which part of the CIA triad was compromised?", "opts": ["Availability, because the records server stopped responding to members during the transfer", "Integrity, because member records were changed while they were being transferred", "Confidentiality, because data appear to have been sent to a known malicious address", "Integrity, because the transfer used port 443 instead of the expected port 22"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Network traffic showing exfiltration of sensitive data is evidence of a confidentiality breach. A 2.3 GB transfer from the records server to a listed malicious address after an attacker logged in fits that pattern. The log shows no outage or changed records.", "set": "S7"}, {"id": 50, "lo": "4.5.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "Which of the following controls would most directly have stopped the activity recorded at 02:16:41 from leading to the login at 02:19:03?", "opts": ["An account lockout after five invalid login attempts", "A guest wireless network with a captive portal", "A static IP address for the member-records server", "A switch port configured as a trunk port"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Account lockout after a set number of invalid attempts keeps an adversary from guessing passwords continuously. The 48 failed attempts would have triggered it long before the login succeeded.", "set": "S7"}, {"id": 51, "lo": "4.2.A", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "A technician finds that a laptop can load a website when the site's IP address is typed into the browser, but not when the site's domain name is entered. Which protocol is most likely failing?", "opts": ["ARP", "DHCP", "DNS", "ICMP"], "ans": "C", "expl": "DNS translates domain names into IP addresses. The laptop has working connectivity (the IP address works), so the name translation step is failing. ARP maps IP addresses to MAC addresses, DHCP assigns addresses, and ICMP is used by ping and traceroute.", "set": null}, {"id": 52, "lo": "4.2.B", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A router uses IP addresses to forward data between networks. At which layer of the OSI model does this occur, and what is the protocol data unit (PDU) at that layer called?", "opts": ["Layer 2, frames", "Layer 3, packets", "Layer 4, segments", "Layer 7, data"], "ans": "B", "expl": "The network layer (layer 3) uses IP addresses and routers, and its PDU is the packet. Layer 2 uses MAC addresses and frames, layer 4 uses TCP and UDP segments, and layer 7 holds application data.", "set": null}, {"id": 53, "lo": "4.2.C", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "Users report that a website will not load. A technician first confirms that the computer's cable is connected and its link light is on, then checks the IP configuration, then checks the firewall rules. Which troubleshooting approach is the technician using?", "opts": ["Bottom-up", "Middle-out", "Outside-in", "Top-down"], "ans": "A", "expl": "A bottom-up approach starts with physical connection checks (layer 1) and moves up through the network layer to the transport layer, where firewalls sit. Top-down begins with applications, and middle-out begins with network settings.", "set": null}, {"id": 54, "lo": "4.3.A", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "A user sees the prompt jlee@ws04 /home/jlee/Documents $ and enters cd .. followed by pwd. Which of the following does pwd display?", "opts": ["/", "/home", "/home/jlee", "/home/jlee/Documents"], "ans": "C", "expl": "cd .. moves one level toward the root, from /home/jlee/Documents to /home/jlee, and pwd prints the current working directory.", "set": null}, {"id": 55, "lo": "4.3.A", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "A user enters the command ls -a /etc on a Linux system. Which of the following correctly identifies the parts of the command?", "opts": ["ls is an argument, -a is the command, and /etc is an option.", "ls is an option, -a is an argument, and /etc is the command.", "ls is the command, -a is an argument, and /etc is an option.", "ls is the command, -a is an option, and /etc is an argument."], "ans": "D", "expl": "CLI commands follow the pattern command, options, arguments. ls is the program, -a is an option (flag) that also lists hidden files, and /etc is the argument naming the directory to list.", "set": null}, {"id": 56, "lo": "4.3.B", "skill": "1.D", "stem": "A technician must download the file router-backup.cfg from the account netops on the server 10.0.8.5 using SFTP. Which of the following sequences of commands accomplishes this?", "opts": ["sftp netops@10.0.8.5, then get router-backup.cfg", "sftp netops@10.0.8.5, then put router-backup.cfg", "sftp 10.0.8.5@netops, then get router-backup.cfg", "sftp 10.0.8.5@netops, then put router-backup.cfg"], "ans": "A", "expl": "An SFTP session is opened with sftp username@host, and get downloads a file from the remote device to the local device. put uploads, which is the wrong direction, and the username comes before the @.", "set": null}, {"id": 57, "lo": "4.4.B", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A router running the Routing Information Protocol (RIP) knows two routes to the network 172.16.50.0/24. Route X has 3 hops over 1 Gbps links. Route Y has 5 hops over 10 Gbps links. Which route will RIP select?", "opts": ["Route X, because RIP chooses the path with the fewest hops", "Route X, because RIP chooses the path with the lowest bandwidth", "Route Y, because RIP chooses the path with the fastest links", "Route Y, because RIP chooses the path with the most routers"], "ans": "A", "expl": "RIP uses hop count as its metric and picks the path with the fewest routers, so Route X (3 hops) wins even though Route Y's links are faster. A protocol that weighs link speed and bandwidth, such as OSPF, might choose differently.", "set": null}, {"id": 58, "lo": "4.5.D", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A technician creates VLAN 30, named CAMERAS, on a managed switch. The store's security cameras are connected to switch ports 9 through 12. Which of the following steps is still required for the cameras to become part of VLAN 30?", "opts": ["Configure each camera to use VLAN 30 as its default gateway.", "Configure ports 9 through 12 as access ports assigned to VLAN 30.", "Configure ports 9 through 12 as trunk ports that carry every VLAN.", "Configure each camera with a public IP address in VLAN 30."], "ans": "B", "expl": "Basic VLAN configuration is create the VLAN, assign its ID, and assign ports. Devices join a VLAN through access ports configured with that VLAN ID. Trunk ports carry multiple VLANs between switches or routers, and a VLAN is not a gateway address.", "set": null}, {"id": 59, "lo": "4.6.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "Which of the following is the correct compressed form of the IPv6 address 2001:0DB8:0000:0000:0000:0040:0000:0A01?", "opts": ["2001:DB8::40::A01", "2001:DB8::40:0:A01", "2001:DB8:0:40:0:A01", "2001:0DB8::0040::0A01"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Leading zeros are removed from each hextet (0DB8 → DB8, 0040 → 40, 0A01 → A01), and one run of all-zero hextets (the three in a row) is replaced by ::. The single zero hextet that remains is written as 0. :: can appear only once in an address, and an address that drops hextets without using :: is invalid.", "set": null}, {"id": 60, "lo": "4.6.B", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "A pharmacy's network loses connectivity whenever brief power outages occur, and its internet service provider has had two multi-hour outages this year. Which pair of measures would most improve the network's availability?", "opts": ["Installing an uninterruptible power supply (UPS) and adding backup cellular internet", "Upgrading the wireless access points and adding a second SSID for pharmacy staff", "Requiring multifactor authentication and enforcing stronger passwords for all staff", "Assigning static IP addresses and creating a separate VLAN for each department"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Redundancy improves reliability: backup power through a UPS covers brief outages, and mobile or satellite internet covers primary ISP outages. The other choices improve security or organization, not availability during these failures.", "set": null}], "frq": {"title": "Cedar Valley Veterinary Clinic", "intro": "<p>You are a network technician for Cedar Valley Veterinary Clinic, which is open Monday through Saturday and closed on Sundays. On Monday morning, the front desk reports that the appointment system shows the wrong vaccination due dates for many pets. The clinic manager asks you to investigate, using the four sources below, and to recommend fixes.</p>", "sources": [["Source 1: Network documentation", "<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Segment</th><th>VLAN</th><th>Subnet</th><th>Devices</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Staff</td><td>10</td><td>192.168.10.0/24</td><td>6 desktops (DHCP), 2 printers (static), IT admin workstation 192.168.10.20 (static)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Records server</td><td>20</td><td>192.168.20.0/28</td><td>Medical records server 192.168.20.5 (static)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Guest Wi-Fi</td><td>30</td><td>192.168.30.0/24</td><td>Waiting-room visitors (DHCP)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>IoT</td><td>40</td><td>192.168.40.0/24</td><td>4 kennel cameras, 1 smart thermostat (DHCP)</td></tr></table>\n<p>Asset note: kennel cameras run firmware v1.0.2 (released 2019); camera administrator passwords have not been changed from the factory default.</p>"], ["Source 2: Firewall ACL for traffic entering the records server segment", "<pre>Rule  Action  Direction  Protocol  Port  Source\n101   ALLOW   inbound    TCP       443   192.168.10.0/24\n102   ALLOW   inbound    TCP       23    192.168.10.0/24\n103   ALLOW   inbound    TCP       ALL   192.168.40.0/24\n104   DENY    inbound    ALL       ALL   ALL</pre>"], ["Source 3: IDS and server log excerpt (Sunday)", "<pre>Time      Source            Destination     Event\n01:12:40  192.168.40.14     192.168.20.5    SSH login failed (user: root) x260\n01:40:02  192.168.40.14     192.168.20.5    SSH login succeeded (user: vetadmin)\n01:44:19  192.168.20.5      -               1,820 records modified (field: vaccination_date)\n01:51:07  192.168.40.14     192.168.10.0/24 Port scan detected (TCP 1-1024)</pre>\n<p>DHCP lease table: 192.168.40.14 is leased to the device named kennel-cam-3.</p>"], ["Source 4: Clinic network policy (excerpt)", "<ol class=\"policy\">\n<li><b>P1 Least privilege.</b> Each device and user may reach only the services needed for its role.</li>\n<li><b>P2 Encrypted administration.</b> Remote administration of any server must use an encrypted protocol.</li>\n<li><b>P3 IoT isolation.</b> IoT devices must not communicate with the records server segment.</li>\n<li><b>P4 Account lockout.</b> Accounts lock after 5 failed login attempts.</li>\n<li><b>P5 Default credentials.</b> Default credentials must be changed before a device joins the network.</li></ol>"]], "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "pts": 2, "lo": "3.5.C, 2.6.A", "prompt": "Identify the rule in Source 2 that violates policy P2, and explain why. Then identify the rule in Source 2 that violates policy P3.", "rubric": ["1 point: Identifies rule 102 and explains that it allows Telnet (port 23), which sends remote CLI sessions, including credentials, unencrypted.", "1 point: Identifies rule 103, which allows all TCP traffic from the IoT segment (192.168.40.0/24) into the records server segment."], "sample": "Rule 102 violates P2 because it allows Telnet on port 23, an unencrypted remote CLI protocol, so administrator credentials and commands could be read if captured. Rule 103 violates P3 because it allows every TCP port from the IoT segment 192.168.40.0/24 into the records server segment."}, {"label": "(b)", "pts": 3, "lo": "4.5.C, 4.1.A", "prompt": "Using Source 3, identify two different indicators of a security threat. Then identify which part of the CIA triad was compromised and cite the specific evidence that supports your answer.", "rubric": ["1 point: Identifies one threat indicator from the log, such as 260 repeated failed SSH logins from 192.168.40.14 or a login outside business hours (01:40 on Sunday).", "1 point: Identifies a second, different indicator, such as the unauthorized port scan of the staff segment at 01:51 or the successful login from a camera, a device that has no reason to log in to the server.", "1 point: Identifies integrity as compromised, citing the 1,820 vaccination_date records modified at 01:44 right after the unauthorized login (and the wrong due dates staff saw on Monday)."], "sample": "One indicator is the 260 failed SSH logins for root from 192.168.40.14 at 01:12, which shows password guessing. A second is the port scan of the staff segment from the same address at 01:51. Integrity was compromised: four minutes after kennel-cam-3 logged in as vetadmin, 1,820 vaccination_date records were modified, which is why the front desk sees wrong due dates."}, {"label": "(c)", "pts": 3, "lo": "3.5.C, 3.4.B", "prompt": "Write a revised ACL for traffic entering the records server segment, using the same format as Source 2. Your ACL must allow staff to reach the records application over HTTPS, allow encrypted remote administration only from the IT admin workstation, and follow policies P1, P2, and P3.", "rubric": ["1 point: Includes an ALLOW rule for TCP 443 from 192.168.10.0/24 (staff).", "1 point: Includes an ALLOW rule for TCP 22 (SSH) whose source is only 192.168.10.20 (or 192.168.10.20/32), and includes no rule allowing port 23 or traffic from 192.168.40.0/24.", "1 point: Ends with a DENY rule for all remaining traffic, placed below every ALLOW rule so the allow rules are reached first (deny-by-default)."], "sample": "<pre>101 ALLOW inbound TCP 22  192.168.10.20\n102 ALLOW inbound TCP 443 192.168.10.0/24\n103 DENY  inbound ALL ALL ALL</pre>The SSH rule is limited to the one admin workstation (least privilege), Telnet and IoT access are gone, and the final rule denies everything else because ACLs are read from top to bottom."}, {"label": "(d)", "pts": 1, "lo": "3.4.C", "prompt": "The clinic will add 9 exam-room tablets on their own subnet, taken from the start of the network 192.168.50.0/24. Determine the smallest appropriate subnet in CIDR notation and the range of usable host addresses.", "rubric": ["1 point: Determines /28 (255.255.255.240) with a usable host range of 192.168.50.1 to 192.168.50.14. Both are required for the point."], "sample": "9 tablets + 2 reserved addresses = 11, and the next power of 2 is 16 (4 host bits), so the subnet is 192.168.50.0/28. The usable range is 192.168.50.1 to 192.168.50.14 (.0 is the network address and .15 is the broadcast address)."}, {"label": "(e)", "pts": 2, "lo": "2.5.B, 4.5.B", "prompt": "Recommend two security controls, other than changing the firewall rules, that would reduce the chance of a similar incident. For each control, justify your recommendation with specific evidence from the sources.", "rubric": ["1 point: Recommends one appropriate control with justification tied to evidence, such as changing the cameras' default passwords (Source 1 says they were never changed; policy P5).", "1 point: Recommends a second, different control with justification tied to evidence, such as updating the 2019 camera firmware, enforcing account lockout after 5 failures (Source 3 shows 260 attempts; policy P4), or monitoring and alerting on after-hours logins."], "sample": "First, change the default administrator passwords on all kennel cameras, because Source 1 shows they were never changed, which breaks P5 and likely let an attacker take over kennel-cam-3. Second, enforce account lockout after 5 failed attempts on the records server: the log shows 260 failed SSH logins with no lockout, so P4 is not being enforced, and lockout would have stopped the guessing long before the 01:40 login."}]}};
